bibliograph (0.5.2) unstable; urgency=medium

  * `requeue-isbn-enrichment.ts` only retries rows whose verdict is older than
    `--older-than-days` (default 30). A retry that decides the same way
    re-stamps the same verdict, so without the guard every run re-asked the
    whole backlog — the first full pass re-queued 60k rows that had just been
    checked.

 -- Bibliograph Maintainers <maintainers@olamaelcu.net>  Fri, 06 Nov 2026 20:52:41 -0500

bibliograph (0.5.1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * The description backfill is scheduled: a daily timer enqueues one bounded
    batch (`--limit 20000`, ~20% of the shared Google Books quota) of
    `backfill-edition-description` jobs that the existing worker drains. The
    scan gained `--dry-run`/`--limit` and releases its pool, and rows it defers
    stay actionable for the next tick.
  * A 429 is no longer recorded as a permanent miss. `description_checked_at`
    is only stamped when no upstream request failed during the lookup, decided
    by a new lifetime-failure counter on the circuit breaker, so rows skipped
    during a quota-exhausted day are retried instead of being lost.
  * Contributor stubs no longer enqueue a publish job. The 0.4.0 OpenLibrary
    removal deleted the `ingest-contributor` task, so every minted stub queued
    a job no handler would ever pick up — one catalog pass left 21,303 inert
    rows — and the per-name insert dominated the walk. The stub row is upserted
    synchronously, which is what serving and matching read; ingest persists
    records in Postgres, exactly as edition ingest already does.

 -- Bibliograph Maintainers <maintainers@olamaelcu.net>  Fri, 06 Nov 2026 19:20:00 -0500

bibliograph (0.5.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * OpenLibrary returns as an ISBN-discovery source only. A title search
    resolves each top work's editions and pairs every edition with its own
    ISBN (work-level results aggregate all editions' ISBNs, which is the wrong
    thing to write); the existing matcher still decides.
  * Goodreads joins the ISBN search: its general source maps no ISBN, so the
    lookup fetches the top hits' pages (three, bounded) and keeps the ones that
    carry one.
  * Bookhive contributors are ingested: the free-form `authors` string now
    mints `bh.a-<slug>` strongRefs (deterministic uri/cid, ingest queued)
    instead of being dropped, so records serve contributors and the ISBN
    matcher has an author to verify. `scripts/backfill-bookhive-catalog.ts`
    fills both ISBNs and contributors on editions imported before the change.
  * `scripts/requeue-isbn-enrichment.ts --result=<shadow|none|ambiguous>`
    re-enqueues rows the daily scan skips because they already have a verdict,
    so the new sources can revisit the backlog.
  * Titles cut subtitles at `[` as well as `:`/`;`/`(`, matching the bracketed
    edition notes OpenLibrary uses.

